#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:2
#, no-c-format
msgid "Developing &krita; Plugins"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:5
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:7
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; is infinitely extensible with plugins. Tools, filters, large chunks "
"of the user interface and even colorspaces are plugins. In fact, &krita; "
"recognizes these six types of plugins:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:14
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"colorspaces &mdash; these define the channels that constitute a single pixel"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:16
#, no-c-format
msgid "tools &mdash; anything that is done with a mouse or tablet input device"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid "paint operations &mdash; pluggable painting effects for tools"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:20
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"image filters &mdash; change all pixels, or just the selected pixels in a "
"layer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:22
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"viewplugins &mdash; extend Krita’s user interface with new dialog boxes, "
"palettes and operations"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:24
#, no-c-format
msgid "import/export filters &mdash; read and write all kinds of image formats"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:28
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; itself consists of three layered libraries and a directory with some "
"common support classes: kritacolor, kritaimage and kritaui. Within &krita;, "
"objects can by identified by a <classname>KisID</classname>, that is the "
"combination of a unique untranslated string (used when saving, for instance) "
"and a translated string for GUI purposes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:34
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A word on compatibility: &krita; is still in development. From &krita; 1.5 "
"to 1.6 not many API changes are expected, but there may be some. From "
"&krita; 1.6 to 2.0 we will move from &Qt;3 to &Qt;4, from &kde;3 to &kde;4, "
"from <command>automake</command> to <command>cmake</command>: many changes "
"are to be expected. If you develop a plugin for &krita; and choose to do so "
"in &krita;’s subversion repository, chances are excellent that we’ll help "
"you porting. These changes may also render parts of this document out of "
"date. Always check with the latest API documentation or the header files "
"installed on your system."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:47
#, no-c-format
msgid "KritaColor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:49
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The first library is kritacolor. This library loads the colorspace plugins."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:51
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A colorspace plugin should implement the <classname>KisColorSpace</"
"classname> abstract class or, if the basic capabilities of the new "
"colorspace will be implemented by <command>lcms</command> (<ulink url="
"\"http://www.littlecms.com/\"></ulink>), extend "
"<classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</classname>. The kritacolor library could "
"be used from other applications and does not depend on &koffice;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid "KritaImage"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:63
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The libkritaimage library loads the filter and paintop plugins and is "
"responsible for working with image data: changing pixels, compositing and "
"painting. Brushes, palettes, gradients and patterns are also loaded by "
"libkritaimage. It is our stated goal to make libkritaimage independent of "
"&koffice;, but we currently share the gradient loading code with &koffice;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is not easy at the moment to add new types of resources such as brushes, "
"palettes, gradients or patterns to &krita;. (Adding new brushes, palettes, "
"gradients and patterns is easy, of course.) &krita; follows the guidelines "
"of the Create project (<ulink url=\"http://create.freedesktop.org/\"></"
"ulink>) for these. Adding support for Photoshop's brush file format needs "
"libkritaimage hacking; adding more gimp brush data files not."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid "<classname>KritaImage</classname> loads the following types of plugins:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; filters must extend and implement the abstract class "
"<classname>KisFilter</classname>, <classname>KisFilterConfiguration</"
"classname> and possibly <classname>KisFilterConfigurationWidget</classname>. "
"An example of a filter is Unsharp Mask."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Paint operations or paintops are the set of operations painting tools suchs "
"as freehand or circle have access to. Examples of paintops are pen, airbrush "
"or eraser. Paintops should extend the <classname>KisPaintop</classname> base "
"class. Examples of new paintops could be a chalk brush, an oilpaint brush or "
"a complex programmable brush."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:97
#, no-c-format
msgid "KritaUI"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:99
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The libkritaui library loads the tool and viewplugins. This library is a "
"&koffice; Part, but also contains a number of widgets that are useful for "
"graphics applications. Maybe we will have to split this library in kritapart "
"and kritaui in the 2.0 release. For now, script writers are not given access "
"to this library and plugin writers are only allowed to use this library when "
"writing tools or viewplugins. <classname>KritaUI</classname> loads the "
"following types of plugins:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tools are derived from <classname>KisTool</classname> or one of the "
"specialized tool base classes such as <classname>KisToolPaint</classname>, "
"<classname>KisToolNonPaint</classname> or <classname>KisToolFreehand</"
"classname>. A new tool could be a foreground object selection tool. Painting "
"tools (and that includes tools that paint on the selection) can use any "
"paintop to determine the way pixels are changed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:117
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Viewplugins are ordinary KParts that use <command>kxmlgui</command> to "
"insinuate themselves into &krita;'s user interface. Menu options, dialogs, "
"toolbars &mdash; any kind of user interface extension can be a viewplugin. "
"In fact, important functionality like &krita;'s scripting support is written "
"as a viewplugin."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:127 developers-plugins.docbook:882
#, no-c-format
msgid "Import/Export filters"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:129
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Import/Export filters are &koffice; filters, subclasses of "
"<classname>KoFilter</classname>. Filters read and write image data in any of "
"the myriad image formats in existence. And example of a new &krita; import/"
"export filter could be a PDF filter. Filters are loaded by the &koffice; "
"libraries."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:142
#, no-c-format
msgid "Creating plugins"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Plugins are written in C++ and can use all of &kde; and &Qt; and the &krita; "
"developer API. Only viewplugins should use the &koffice; API. Don’t worry: "
"&krita;’s API’s are quite clear and rather extensively documented (for free "
"software) and coding your first filter is really easy."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:149
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you do not want to use C++, you can write scripts in Python or Ruby; that "
"is a different thing altogether, though, and you cannot currently write "
"tools, colorspaces, paintops or import/export filters as scripts."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:153
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; plugins use &kde;'s parts mechanism for loading, so the parts "
"documentation at <ulink url=\"http://developer.kde.org\"></ulink> is "
"relevant here, too."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Your distribution should have either installed the relevant header files "
"with &krita; itself, or might have split the header files into either a "
"&koffice; dev or a &krita; dev package. You can find the API documentation "
"for &krita;'s public API at <ulink url=\"http://koffice.org/developer/"
"apidocs/krita/html/\"></ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid "Automake (and CMake)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:166
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kde; 3.x and thus &koffice; 1.5 and 1.6 use <command>automake</command>; "
"&kde; 4.0 and &koffice; 2.0 use <command>cmake</command>. This tutorial "
"describes the <command>automake</command> way of creating plugins."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:172
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Plugins are &kde; modules and should be tagged as such in their "
"<filename>Makefile.am</filename>. Filters, tools, paintops, colorspaces and "
"import/export filters need <literal role=\"extension\">.desktop</literal> "
"files; viewplugins need a <application>KXMLGui</application> "
"<filename>pluginname.rc</filename> file in addition. The easiest way to get "
"started is to checkout the krita-plugins project from the &koffice; "
"Subversion repository and use it as the basis for your own project. We "
"intend to prepare a skeleton &krita; plugin pack for KDevelop, but haven’t "
"had the time to do so yet."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: filename
#: developers-plugins.docbook:185
#, no-c-format
msgid "Makefile.am"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:187
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let's look at the skeleton for a plugin module. First, the "
"<filename>Makefile.am</filename>. This is what &kde; uses to generate the "
"makefile that builds your plugin: <programlisting>\n"
"kde_services_DATA = kritaLIBRARYNAME.desktop\n"
"\n"
"INCLUDES = $(all_includes)\n"
"\n"
"kritaLIBRARYNAME_la_SOURCES = sourcefile1.cc sourcefile2.cc\n"
"\n"
"kde_module_LTLIBRARIES = kritaLIBRARYNAME.la\n"
"noinst_HEADERS = header1.h header2.h\n"
"\n"
"kritaLIBRARYNAME_la_LDFLAGS = $(all_libraries) -module $(KDE_PLUGIN)\n"
"kritaLIBRARY_la_LIBADD = -lkritacommon\n"
"\n"
"kritaextensioncolorsfilters_la_METASOURCES = AUTO\n"
"</programlisting> This is the makefile for a filter plugin. Replace "
"<replaceable>LIBRARYNAME</replaceable> with the name of your work, and you "
"are set."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If your plugin is a viewplugin, you will likely also install a <literal role="
"\"extension\">.rc</literal> file with entries for menubars and toolbars. "
"Likewise, you may need to install cursors and icons. That is all done "
"through the ordinary &kde; <filename>Makefile.am</filename> magic "
"incantantions:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:203
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"kritarcdir = $(kde_datadir)/krita/kritaplugins\n"
"kritarc_DATA = LIBRARYNAME.rc\n"
"EXTRA_DIST = $(kritarc_DATA)\n"
"\n"
"kritapics_DATA = \\\n"
"   bla.png \\\n"
"   bla_cursor.png\n"
"kritapicsdir = $(kde_datadir)/krita/pics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:209
#, no-c-format
msgid "Desktop files"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:211
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <literal role=\"extension\">.desktop</literal> file announces the type "
"of plugin:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"[Desktop Entry]\n"
"Encoding=UTF-8\n"
"Icon=\n"
"Name=User-visible Name\n"
"ServiceTypes=Krita/Filter\n"
"Type=Service\n"
"X-KDE-Library=kritaLIBRARYNAME\n"
"X-KDE-Version=2"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:215
#, no-c-format
msgid "Possible ServiceTypes are:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:220
#, no-c-format
msgid "Krita/Filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:221
#, no-c-format
msgid "Krita/Paintop"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:222
#, no-c-format
msgid "Krita/ViewPlugin"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:223
#, no-c-format
msgid "Krita/Tool"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:224
#, no-c-format
msgid "Krita/ColorSpace"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:227
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"File import and export filters use the generic &koffice; filter framework "
"and need to be discussed separately."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid "Boilerplate"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:236
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You also need a bit of boilerplate code that is called by the &kde; part "
"framework to instantiate the plugin &mdash; a header file and an "
"implementation file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:239
#, no-c-format
msgid "A header file:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:241
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#ifndef TOOL_STAR_H_\n"
"#define TOOL_STAR_H_\n"
"\n"
"#include &lt;kparts/plugin.h&gt;\n"
"\n"
"/**\n"
"* A module that provides a star tool.\n"
"*/\n"
"class ToolStar : public KParts::Plugin\n"
"{\n"
"   Q_OBJECT\n"
"public:\n"
"   ToolStar(QObject *parent, const char *name, const QStringList &amp;);\n"
"   virtual ~ToolStar();\n"
"\n"
"};\n"
"\n"
"#endif // TOOL_STAR_H_"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:244
#, no-c-format
msgid "And an implementation file:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#include &lt;kinstance.h&gt;\n"
"#include &lt;kgenericfactory.h&gt;\n"
"\n"
"#include &lt;kis_tool_registry.h&gt;\n"
"\n"
"#include \"tool_star.h\"\n"
"#include \"kis_tool_star.h\"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"typedef KGenericFactory&lt;ToolStar&gt; ToolStarFactory;\n"
"K_EXPORT_COMPONENT_FACTORY( kritatoolstar, ToolStarFactory( \"krita\" ) )\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"ToolStar::ToolStar(QObject *parent, const char *name, const QStringList "
"&amp;)\n"
"   : KParts::Plugin(parent, name)\n"
"{\n"
"   setInstance(ToolStarFactory::instance());\n"
"   if ( parent->inherits(\"KisToolRegistry\") )\n"
"   {\n"
"       KisToolRegistry * r = dynamic_cast&lt;KisToolRegistry*&gt;"
"( parent );\n"
"       r -> add(new KisToolStarFactory());\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"ToolStar::~ToolStar()\n"
"{\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"#include \"tool_star.moc\""
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:251
#, no-c-format
msgid "Registries"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:253
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tools are loaded by the tool registry and register themselves with the tool "
"registry. Plugins like tools, filters and paintops are loaded only once: "
"view plugins are loaded for every view that is created. Note that we "
"register factories, generally speaking. For instance, with tools a new "
"instance of a tool is created for every pointer (mouse, stylus, eraser) for "
"every few. And a new paintop is created whenever a tool gets a mouse-down "
"event."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:262
#, no-c-format
msgid "Filters call the filter registry:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:264
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if (parent->inherits(\"KisFilterRegistry\")) {\n"
"       KisFilterRegistry * manager = dynamic_cast&lt;KisFilterRegistry *&gt;"
"(parent);\n"
"       manager->add(new KisFilterInvert());\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:265
#, no-c-format
msgid "Paintops the paintop registry:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:267
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if ( parent->inherits(\"KisPaintOpRegistry\") ) {\n"
"           KisPaintOpRegistry * r = dynamic_cast&lt;KisPaintOpRegistry*&gt;"
"(parent);\n"
"           r -> add ( new KisSmearyOpFactory );\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:268
#, no-c-format
msgid "Colorspaces the colorspace registry (with some complications):"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:270
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if ( parent->inherits(\"KisColorSpaceFactoryRegistry\") ) {\n"
"        KisColorSpaceFactoryRegistry * f = dynamic_cast&lt;"
"isColorSpaceFactoryRegistry*&gt;(parent);\n"
"\n"
"       KisProfile *defProfile = new KisProfile(cmsCreate_sRGBProfile());\n"
"       f->addProfile(defProfile);\n"
"\n"
"       KisColorSpaceFactory * csFactory = new KisRgbColorSpaceFactory();\n"
"       f->add(csFactory);\n"
"   \n"
"       KisColorSpace * colorSpaceRGBA = new KisRgbColorSpace(f, 0);\n"
"       KisHistogramProducerFactoryRegistry::instance() -> add(\n"
"               new KisBasicHistogramProducerFactory&lt;"
"KisBasicU8HistogramProducer&gt;\n"
"               (KisID(\"RGB8HISTO\", i18n(\"RGB8 Histogram\")), "
"colorSpaceRGBA) );\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:271
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"View plugins do not have to register themselves, and they get access to a "
"<classname>KisView</classname> object:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:274
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if ( parent->inherits(\"KisView\") )\n"
"   {\n"
"       setInstance(ShearImageFactory::instance());\n"
"       setXMLFile(locate(\"data\",\"kritaplugins/shearimage.rc\"), true);\n"
"\n"
"       (void) new KAction(i18n(\"&amp;Shear Image...\"), 0, 0, this, SLOT"
"(slotShearImage()), actionCollection(), \"shearimage\");\n"
"       (void) new KAction(i18n(\"&amp;Shear Layer...\"), 0, 0, this, SLOT"
"(slotShearLayer()), actionCollection(), \"shearlayer\");\n"
"\n"
"       m_view = (KisView*) parent;\n"
"   }"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:275
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Remember that this means that a view plugin will be created for every view "
"the user creates: splitting a view means loading all view plugins again."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:282
#, no-c-format
msgid "Plugin versioning"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:284
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; 1.5 loads plugins with <literal>X-KDE-Version=2</literal> set in the "
"<literal role=\"extension\">.desktop</literal> file. &krita; 1.6 plugins "
"will probably be binary incompatible with 1.5 plugins and will need the "
"version number 3. &krita; 2.0 plugins will need the version number 3. Yes, "
"this is not entirely logical."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:297
#, no-c-format
msgid "Colorspaces"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:299
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Colorspaces implement the <classname>KisColorSpace</classname> pure virtual "
"class. There are two types of colorspaces: those that can use <command>lcms</"
"command> for transformations between colorspaces, and those that are too "
"weird for <command>lcms</command> to handle. Examples of the first are cmyk, "
"rgb, yuv. An example of the latter is watercolor or wet &amp; sticky. "
"Colorspaces that use <command>lcms</command> can be derived from "
"<classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</classname>, or of one of the base classes "
"that are specialized for a certain number of bits per channel."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:308
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Implementing a colorspace is pretty easy. The general principle is that "
"colorspaces work on a simple array of bytes. The interpretation of these "
"bytes is up to the colorspace. For instance, a pixel in 16-bit GrayA "
"consists of four bytes: two bytes for the gray value and two bytes for the "
"alpha value. You are free to use a struct to work with the memory layout of "
"a pixel in your colorspace implementation, but that representation is not "
"exported. The only way the rest of &krita; can know what channels and types "
"of channels your colorspace pixels consist of is through the "
"<classname>KisChannelInfo</classname> class."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:318
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Filters and paintops make use of the rich set of methods offered by "
"<classname>KisColorSpace</classname> to do their work. In many cases, the "
"default implementation in <classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</classname> will "
"work, but more slowly than a custom implementation in your own colorspace "
"because <classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</classname> will convert all pixels "
"to 16-bit L*a*b and back."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: classname
#: developers-plugins.docbook:328
#, no-c-format
msgid "KisChannelInfo"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:330
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"(http://websvn.kde.org/trunk/koffice/krita/kritacolor/kis_channelinfo.h)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:331
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This class defines the channels that make up a single pixel in a particular "
"colorspace. A channel has the following important characteristics:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid "a name for display in the user interface"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"a position: the byte where the bytes representing this channel start in the "
"pixel."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:339
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"a type: color, alpha, substance or substrate. Color is plain color, alpha is "
"see-throughishness, substance is a representation of amount of pigment or "
"things like that, substrate is the representation of the canvas. (Note that "
"this may be refactored at the drop of a hat.)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:343
#, no-c-format
msgid "a valuetype: byte, short, integer, float — or other."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:345
#, no-c-format
msgid "size: the number of bytes this channel takes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:346
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"color: a <classname>QColor</classname> representation of this channel for "
"user interface visualization, for instance in histograms."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:349
#, no-c-format
msgid "an abbreviation for use in the GUI when there’s not much space"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: classname
#: developers-plugins.docbook:355
#, no-c-format
msgid "KisCompositeOp"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:357
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As per original Porter-Duff, there are many ways of combining pixels to get "
"a new color. The <classname>KisCompositeOp</classname> class defines most of "
"them: this set is not easily extensible except by hacking the kritacolor "
"library."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:362
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A colorspace plugin can support any subset of these possible composition "
"operations, but the set must always include \"OVER\" (same as \"NORMAL\") "
"and \"COPY\". The rest are more or less optional, although more is better, "
"of course."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: classname
#: developers-plugins.docbook:371
#, no-c-format
msgid "KisColorSpace"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:373
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The methods in the <classname>KisColorSpace</classname> pure virtual class "
"can be divided into a number of groups: conversion, identification and "
"manipulation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:377
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All classes must be able to convert a pixel from and to 8 bit RGB (i.e., a "
"<classname>QColor</classname>), and preferably also to and from 16 bit "
"L*a*b. Additionally, there is a method to convert to any colorspace from the "
"current colorspace."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:382
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Colorspaces are described by the <classname>KisChannelInfo</classname> "
"vector, number of channels, number of bytes in a single pixel, whether it "
"supports High Dynamic Range images and more."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:386
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Manipulation is for instance the combining of two pixels in a new pixel: "
"bitBlt, darkening or convolving of pixels."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:389
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Please consult the API documentation for a full description of all methods "
"you need to implement in a colorspace."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:392
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</classname> implements many of the virtual "
"methods of <classname>KisColorSpace</classname> using functions from the "
"<command>lcms</command> library. On top of <classname>KisAbstractColorSpace</"
"classname> there are base colorspace classes for 8 and 16 bit integer and 16 "
"and 32 bit float colorspaces that define common operations to move between "
"bit depths."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:405
#, no-c-format
msgid "Filters"
msgstr "Filtres"

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:407
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Filters are plugins that examine the pixels in a layer and them make changes "
"to them. Although &krita; uses an efficient tiled memory backend to store "
"pixels, filter writers do not have to bother with that. When writing a "
"filter plugin for the &Java; imaging API, Photoshop or The Gimp, you need to "
"take care of tile edges and <quote>cobble</quote> tiles together: &krita; "
"hides that implementation detail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:415
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that it is theoretically easy to replace the current tile image data "
"storage backend with another backend, but that backends are not true plugins "
"at the moment, for performance reasons."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:418
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; uses iterators to read and write pixel values. Alternatively, you "
"can read a block of pixels into a memory buffer, mess with it and then write "
"it back as a block. But that is not necessarily more efficient, it may even "
"be slower than using the iterators; it may just be more convenient. See the "
"API documentation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:424
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; images are composed of layers, of which there are currently four "
"kinds: paint layers, group layers, adjustment layers (that contain a filter "
"that is applied dynamically to layers below the adjustment layer) and part "
"layers. Filters always operate on paint layers. Paint layers contain paint "
"devices, of the class <classname>KisPaintDevice</classname>. A paint device "
"in its turn gives access to the actual pixels."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:431
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<classname>PaintDevice</classname>s are generally passed around wrapped in "
"shared pointers. A shared pointer keeps track of in how many places the "
"paint device is currently used and deletes the paint device when it is no "
"longer used anywhere. You recognize the shared pointer version of a paint "
"device through its <literal>SP</literal> suffix. Just remember that you "
"never have to explicitly delete a <classname>KisPaintDeviceSP</classname>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:438
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let's examine a very simple filter, one that inverts every pixel. The code "
"for this filter is in the <filename class=\"directory\">koffice/krita/"
"plugins/filters/example</filename> directory. The main method is "
"<programlisting>\n"
"KisFilterInvert::process(KisPaintDeviceSP src, KisPaintDeviceSP dst,\n"
"                        KisFilterConfiguration* /*config*/, const QRect&amp; "
"rect).\n"
"</programlisting> The function gets passed two paint devices, a "
"configuration object (which is not used in this simple filter) and a "
"<varname>rect</varname>. The <varname>rect</varname> describes the area of "
"the paint device which the filter should act on. This area is described by "
"integers, which means no sub-pixel accuracy."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:449
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <varname>src</varname> paint device is for reading from, the "
"<varname>dst</varname> paint device for writing to. These parameters may "
"point to the same actual paint device, or be two different paint devices. "
"(Note: this may change to only one paint device in the future.)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:454
#, no-c-format
msgid "Now, let's look at the code line by line:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:457
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"void KisFilterInvert::process(KisPaintDeviceSP src, KisPaintDeviceSP dst,\n"
"                             KisFilterConfiguration* /*config*/, const "
"QRect&amp; rect)\n"
"{\n"
"   Q_ASSERT(src != 0);\n"
"   Q_ASSERT(dst != 0);\n"
"\n"
"   KisRectIteratorPixel srcIt = src->createRectIterator(rect.x(), rect.y(), "
"rect.width(), rect.height(), false); <co id=\"invert1\" />\n"
"   KisRectIteratorPixel dstIt = dst->createRectIterator(rect.x(), rect.y(), "
"rect.width(), rect.height(), true ); <co id=\"invert2\" />\n"
"\n"
"   int pixelsProcessed = 0;\n"
"   setProgressTotalSteps(rect.width() * rect.height());\n"
"\n"
"   KisColorSpace * cs = src->colorSpace();\n"
"   qint32 psize = cs->pixelSize();\n"
"\n"
"   while( ! srcIt.isDone() )\n"
"   {\n"
"       if(srcIt.isSelected()) <co id=\"invert3\" />\n"
"       {\n"
"           memcpy(dstIt.rawData(), srcIt.oldRawData(), psize); <co id="
"\"invert4\" />\n"
"\n"
"           cs->invertColor( dstIt.rawData(), 1); <co id=\"invert5\" />\n"
"       }\n"
"       setProgress(++pixelsProcessed);\n"
"       ++srcIt;\n"
"       ++dstIt;\n"
"   }\n"
"   setProgressDone(); // Must be called even if you don't really support "
"progression\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:461
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This creates an iterator to read the existing pixels. Krita has three types "
"of iterators: horizontal, vertical and rectangular. The rect iterator takes "
"the most efficient path through the image data, but does not guarantee "
"anything about the location of the next pixel it returns. That means that "
"you cannot be sure that the pixel you will retrieve next will be adjacent to "
"the pixel you just got. The horizontal and vertical line iterators do "
"guarantee the location of the pixels they return."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:469
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"(2) We create the destination iterator with the <literal>write</literal> "
"setting to <literal>true</literal>. This means that if the destination paint "
"device is smaller than the rect we write, it will automatically be enlarged "
"to fit every pixel we iterate over. Note that we have got a potential bug "
"here: if <varname>dst</varname> and <varname>src</varname> are not the same "
"device, then it is quite possible that the pixels returned by the iterators "
"do not correspond. For every position in the iterator, <varname>src</"
"varname> may be, for example, at 165,200, while <varname>dst</varname> could "
"be at 20,8 &mdash; and therefore the copy we perform below may distort the "
"image..."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:480
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Want to know if a pixel is selected? That is easy &mdash; use the "
"<methodname>isSelected</methodname> method. But selectedness is not a binary "
"property of a pixel, a pixel can be half selected, barely selected or almost "
"completely selected. That value you can also got from the iterator. "
"Selections are actually a mask paint device with a range between 0 and 255, "
"where 0 is completely unselected and 255 completely selected. The iterator "
"has two methods: <methodname>isSelected()</methodname> and "
"<methodname>selectedNess()</methodname>. The first returns true if a pixel "
"is selected to any extent (i.e., the mask value is greater than 1), the "
"other returns the maskvalue."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:492
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As noted above, this <literal>memcpy</literal> is a big bad bug... "
"<methodname>rawData()</methodname> returns the array of bytes which is the "
"current state of the pixel; <methodname>oldRawData()</methodname> returns "
"the array of bytes as it was before we created the iterator. However, we may "
"be copying the wrong pixel here. In actual practice, that will not happen "
"too often, unless <varname>dst</varname> already exists and is not aligned "
"with <varname>src</varname>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:501
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"But this is correct: instead of figuring out which byte represents which "
"channel, we use a function supplied by all colorspaces to invert the current "
"pixel. The colorspaces have a lot of pixel operations you can make use of."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:508
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is not all there is to creating a filter. Filters have two other "
"important components: a configuration object and a configuration widget. The "
"two interact closely. The configuration widget creates a configuration "
"object, but can also be filled from a pre-existing configuration object. "
"Configuration objects can represtent themselves as XML and can be created "
"from XML. That is what makes adjustment layers possible."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:518
#, no-c-format
msgid "Iterators"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:520
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are three types of iterators:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:525
#, no-c-format
msgid "Horizontal lines"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:526
#, no-c-format
msgid "Vertical lines"
msgstr "Linhas verticalas"

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:527
#, no-c-format
msgid "Rectangular iterors"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:530
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The horizontal and vertical line iterators have a method to move the "
"iterator to the next row or column: <methodname>nextRow()</methodname> and "
"<methodname>nextCol()</methodname>. Using these is much faster than creating "
"a new iterator for every line or column."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:535
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Iterators are thread-safe in &krita;, so it is possible to divide the work "
"over multiple threads. However, future versions of &krita; will use the "
"<methodname>supportsThreading()</methodname> method to determine whether "
"your filter can be applied to chunks of the image (&ie;, all pixels modified "
"independently, instead of changed by some value determined from an "
"examination of all pixels in the image) and automatically thread the "
"execution your filter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: classname
#: developers-plugins.docbook:547
#, no-c-format
msgid "KisFilterConfiguration"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:549
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<classname>KisFilterConfiguration</classname> is a structure that is used to "
"save filter settings to disk, for instance for adjustment layers. The "
"scripting plugin uses the property map that’s at the back of "
"<classname>KisFilterConfigaration</classname> to make it possible to script "
"filters. Filters can provide a custom widget that &krita; will show in the "
"filters gallery, the filter preview dialog or the tool option tab of the "
"paint-with-filters tool."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:558
#, no-c-format
msgid "An example, taken from the oilpaint effect filter:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:561
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"class KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration : public KisFilterConfiguration\n"
"{\n"
"\n"
"public:\n"
"\n"
"   KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration(quint32 brushSize, quint32 smooth)\n"
"       : KisFilterConfiguration( \"oilpaint\", 1 )\n"
"       {\n"
"           setProperty(\"brushSize\", brushSize);\n"
"           setProperty(\"smooth\", smooth);\n"
"       };\n"
"public:\n"
"\n"
"   inline quint32 brushSize() { return getInt(\"brushSize\"); };\n"
"   inline quint32 smooth() {return getInt(\"smooth\"); };\n"
"\n"
"};"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: classname
#: developers-plugins.docbook:565
#, no-c-format
msgid "KisFilterConfigurationWidget"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:567
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Most filters can be tweaked by the user. You can create a configuration "
"widget that Krita will use where-ever your filter is used. An example:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: developers-plugins.docbook:574
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The <guilabel>Oilpaint</guilabel> dialog</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: developers-plugins.docbook:580
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The <guilabel>Oilpaint</guilabel> dialog</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:582
#, no-c-format
msgid "<para>The <guilabel>Oilpaint</guilabel> dialog</para>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:587
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that only the left-hand side of this dialog is your responsibility: "
"&krita; takes care of the rest. There are three ways of going about creating "
"an option widget:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:594
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use &Qt; Designer to create a widget base, and subclass it for your filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:596
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Use one of the simple widgets that show a number of sliders for lists of "
"integers, doubles or bools. These are useful if, like the above screenshot, "
"your filter can be configured by a number of integers, doubles or bools. See "
"the API dox for <classname>KisMultiIntegerFilterWidget</classname>, "
"<classname>KisMultiDoubleFilterWidget</classname> and "
"<classname>KisMultiBoolFilterWidget</classname>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:602
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Hand-code a widget. This is not recommended, and if you do so and want your "
"filter to become part of &krita;’s official release, then I’ll ask you to "
"replate your hand-coded widget with a &Qt; Designer widget."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:608
#, no-c-format
msgid "The oilpaint filter uses the multi integer widget:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:612
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KisFilterConfigWidget * KisOilPaintFilter::createConfigurationWidget"
"(QWidget* parent, KisPaintDeviceSP /*dev*/)\n"
"{\n"
"   vKisIntegerWidgetParam param;\n"
"   param.push_back( KisIntegerWidgetParam( 1, 5, 1, i18n(\"Brush size\"), "
"\"brushSize\" ) );\n"
"   param.push_back( KisIntegerWidgetParam( 10, 255, 30, i18n(\"Smooth\"), "
"\"smooth\" ) );\n"
"   return new KisMultiIntegerFilterWidget(parent, id().id().ascii(), id().id"
"().ascii(), param );\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"KisFilterConfiguration* KisOilPaintFilter::configuration(QWidget* nwidget)\n"
"{\n"
"   KisMultiIntegerFilterWidget* widget = (KisMultiIntegerFilterWidget*) "
"nwidget;\n"
"   if( widget == 0 )\n"
"   {\n"
"       return new KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration( 1, 30);\n"
"   } else {\n"
"       return new KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration( widget->valueAt( 0 ), "
"widget->valueAt( 1 ) );\n"
"   }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"std::list&lt;KisFilterConfiguration*&gt; KisOilPaintFilter::"
"listOfExamplesConfiguration(KisPaintDeviceSP )\n"
"{\n"
"   std::list&lt;KisFilterConfiguration*&gt; list;\n"
"   list.insert(list.begin(), new KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration( 1, 30));\n"
"   return list;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:614
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can see how it works: fill a vector with your integer parameters and "
"create the widget. The <methodname>configuration()</methodname> method "
"inspects the widget and creates the right filter configuration object, in "
"this case, of course, <classname>KisOilPaintFilterConfiguration</classname>. "
"The <methodname>listOfExamplesConfiguration</methodname> method (which "
"should be renamed to correct English...) returns a list with example "
"configuration objects for the filters gallery dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:626
#, no-c-format
msgid "Filters conclusion"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:628
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There’s more to coding interesting filters, of course, but with this "
"explanation, the API documentation and access to our source code, you should "
"be able to get started. Don’t hesitate to contact the &krita; developers on "
"IRC or on the mailing list."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:639
#, no-c-format
msgid "Tools"
msgstr "Espleches"

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:641
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tools appear in &krita;’s toolbox. This means that there is limited space "
"for new tools &mdash; think carefully whether a paint operation isn’t enough "
"for your purposes. Tools can use the mouse/tablet and keyboard in complex "
"ways, which paint operations cannot. This is the reason that Duplicate is a "
"tool, but airbrush a paint operation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:647
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Be careful with static data in your tool: a new instance of your tool is "
"created for every input device: mouse, stylus, eraser, airbrush &mdash; "
"whatever. Tools come divided into logical groups:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:653
#, no-c-format
msgid "shape drawing tools (circle, rect)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:654
#, no-c-format
msgid "freehand drawing tools (brush)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:655
#, no-c-format
msgid "transform tools that mess up the geometry of a layer"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:657
#, no-c-format
msgid "fill tools (like bucket fill or gradient)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:658
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"view tools (that don’t change pixels, but alter the way you view the canvas, "
"such as zoom)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:660
#, no-c-format
msgid "select tools (that change the selection mask)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:664
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The tool interface is described in the API documentation for "
"<classname>KisTool</classname>. There are three subclasses: "
"<classname>KisToolPaint</classname>, <classname>KisToolNonPaint</classname> "
"and <classname>KisToolShape</classname> (which is actually a subclass of "
"<classname>KisToolPaint</classname>) that specialize <classname>KisTool</"
"classname> for painting tasks (i.e., changing pixels) , non-painting tasks "
"and shape painting tasks."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:672
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A tool has an option widget, just like filters. Currently, the option "
"widgets are shown in a tab in a dock window. We may change that to a strip "
"under the main menu (which then replaces the toolbar) for &krita; 2.0, but "
"for now, design your option widget to fit in a tab. As always, it’s best to "
"use &Qt; Designer for the design of the option widget."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:678
#, no-c-format
msgid "A good example of a tool is the star tool:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screen
#: developers-plugins.docbook:682
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"kis_tool_star.cc       Makefile.am   tool_star_cursor.png  wdg_tool_star.ui\n"
"kis_tool_star.h        Makefile.in   tool_star.h           \n"
"kritatoolstar.desktop  tool_star.cc  tool_star.png"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:684
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As you see, you need two images: one for the cursor and one for the toolbox. "
"<filename>tool_star.cc</filename> is just the plugin loader, similar to what "
"we have seen above. The real meat is in the implementation:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:690
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KisToolStar::KisToolStar()\n"
"   : KisToolShape(i18n(\"Star\")),\n"
"     m_dragging (false),\n"
"     m_currentImage (0)\n"
"{\n"
"   setName(\"tool_star\");\n"
"   setCursor(KisCursor::load(\"tool_star_cursor.png\", 6, 6));\n"
"   m_innerOuterRatio=40;\n"
"   m_vertices=5;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:692
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The constructor sets the internal name &mdash; which is not translated "
"&mdash; and the call to the superclass sets the visible name. We also load "
"the cursor image and set a number of variables."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:698
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"void KisToolStar::update (KisCanvasSubject *subject)\n"
"{\n"
"   KisToolShape::update (subject);\n"
"   if (m_subject)\n"
"       m_currentImage = m_subject->currentImg();\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:700
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>update()</methodname> method is called when the tool is "
"selected. This is not a <classname>KisTool</classname> method, but a "
"<classname>KisCanvasObserver</classname> method. Canvas observers are "
"notified whenever something changes in the view, which can be useful for "
"tools."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:705
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The following methods (<methodname>buttonPress</methodname>, "
"<methodname>move</methodname> and <methodname>buttonRelease</methodname>) "
"are called by &krita; when the input device (mouse, stylus, eraser etc.) is "
"pressed down, moved or released. Note that you also get move events if the "
"mouse button isn’t pressed. The events are not the regular &Qt; events, but "
"synthetic &krita; events because we make use of low-level trickery to get "
"enough events to draw a smooth line. By default, toolkits like &Qt; (and "
"GTK) drop events if they are too busy to handle them, and we want them all."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:716
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"void KisToolStar::buttonPress(KisButtonPressEvent *event)\n"
"{\n"
"   if (m_currentImage &amp;&amp; event->button() == LeftButton) {\n"
"       m_dragging = true;\n"
"       m_dragStart = event->pos();\n"
"       m_dragEnd = event->pos();\n"
"       m_vertices = m_optWidget->verticesSpinBox->value();\n"
"       m_innerOuterRatio = m_optWidget->ratioSpinBox->value();\n"
"   }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"void KisToolStar::move(KisMoveEvent *event)\n"
"{\n"
"   if (m_dragging) {\n"
"       // erase old lines on canvas\n"
"       draw(m_dragStart, m_dragEnd);\n"
"       // move (alt) or resize star\n"
"       if (event->state() &amp; Qt::AltButton) {\n"
"           KisPoint trans = event->pos() - m_dragEnd;\n"
"           m_dragStart += trans;\n"
"           m_dragEnd += trans;\n"
"       } else {\n"
"           m_dragEnd = event->pos();\n"
"       }\n"
"       // draw new lines on canvas\n"
"       draw(m_dragStart, m_dragEnd);\n"
"   }\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"void KisToolStar::buttonRelease(KisButtonReleaseEvent *event)\n"
"{\n"
"   if (!m_subject || !m_currentImage)\n"
"       return;\n"
"\n"
"   if (m_dragging &amp;&amp; event->button() == LeftButton) {\n"
"       // erase old lines on canvas\n"
"       draw(m_dragStart, m_dragEnd);\n"
"       m_dragging = false;\n"
"\n"
"       if (m_dragStart == m_dragEnd)\n"
"           return;\n"
"\n"
"       if (!m_currentImage)\n"
"           return;\n"
"\n"
"       if (!m_currentImage->activeDevice())\n"
"           return;\n"
"       \n"
"       KisPaintDeviceSP device = m_currentImage->activeDevice ();;\n"
"       KisPainter painter (device);\n"
"       if (m_currentImage->undo()) painter.beginTransaction (i18n(\"Star"
"\"));\n"
"\n"
"       painter.setPaintColor(m_subject->fgColor());\n"
"       painter.setBackgroundColor(m_subject->bgColor());\n"
"       painter.setFillStyle(fillStyle());\n"
"       painter.setBrush(m_subject->currentBrush());\n"
"       painter.setPattern(m_subject->currentPattern());\n"
"       painter.setOpacity(m_opacity);\n"
"       painter.setCompositeOp(m_compositeOp);\n"
"       KisPaintOp * op =\n"
"           KisPaintOpRegistry::instance()->paintOp(m_subject->currentPaintop"
"(), m_subject->currentPaintopSettings(), &amp;painter);\n"
"       painter.setPaintOp(op); // Painter takes ownership\n"
"\n"
"       vKisPoint coord = starCoordinates(m_vertices, m_dragStart.x(), "
"m_dragStart.y(), m_dragEnd.x(), m_dragEnd.y());\n"
"\n"
"       painter.paintPolygon(coord);\n"
"       \n"
"       device->setDirty( painter.dirtyRect() );\n"
"       notifyModified();\n"
"\n"
"       if (m_currentImage->undo()) {\n"
"           m_currentImage->undoAdapter()->addCommand(painter.endTransaction"
"());\n"
"       }\n"
"   }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:718
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>draw()</methodname> method is an internal method of "
"<classname>KisToolStar</classname> and draws the outline of the star. We "
"call this from the <methodname>move()</methodname> method to give the user "
"feedback of the size and shape of their star. Note that we use the "
"<varname>Qt::NotROP</varname> raster operation, which means that calling "
"<methodname>draw()</methodname> a second time with the same start and end "
"point the previously drawn star will be deleted."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:728
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"void KisToolStar::draw(const KisPoint&amp; start, const KisPoint&amp; end )\n"
"{\n"
"   if (!m_subject || !m_currentImage)\n"
"       return;\n"
"\n"
"   KisCanvasController *controller = m_subject->canvasController();\n"
"   KisCanvas *canvas = controller->kiscanvas();\n"
"   KisCanvasPainter p (canvas);\n"
"   QPen pen(Qt::SolidLine);\n"
"\n"
"   KisPoint startPos;\n"
"   KisPoint endPos;\n"
"   startPos = controller->windowToView(start);\n"
"   endPos = controller->windowToView(end);\n"
"\n"
"   p.setRasterOp(Qt::NotROP);\n"
"\n"
"   vKisPoint points = starCoordinates(m_vertices, startPos.x(), startPos.y"
"(), endPos.x(), endPos.y());\n"
"\n"
"   for (uint i = 0; i &lt; points.count() - 1; i++) {\n"
"       p.drawLine(points[i].floorQPoint(), points[i + 1].floorQPoint());\n"
"   }\n"
"   p.drawLine(points[points.count() - 1].floorQPoint(), points[0].floorQPoint"
"());\n"
"\n"
"   p.end ();\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:730
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>setup()</methodname> method is essential: here we create the "
"action that will be plugged into the toolbox so users can actually select "
"the tool. We also assign a shortcut key. Note that there’s some hackery "
"going on: remember that we create an instance of the tool for every input "
"device. This also means that we call <methodname>setup()</methodname> for "
"every input device and that means that an action with the same name is added "
"several times to the action collection. However, everything seems to work, "
"so why worry?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:740
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"void KisToolStar::setup(KActionCollection *collection)\n"
"{\n"
"   m_action = static_cast&lt;KRadioAction *&gt;(collection->action(name"
"()));\n"
"\n"
"   if (m_action == 0) {\n"
"       KShortcut shortcut(Qt::Key_Plus);\n"
"       shortcut.append(KShortcut(Qt::Key_F9));\n"
"       m_action = new KRadioAction(i18n(\"&amp;Star\"),\n"
"                                   \"tool_star\",\n"
"                                   shortcut,\n"
"                                   this,\n"
"                                   SLOT(activate()),\n"
"                                   collection,\n"
"                                   name());\n"
"       Q_CHECK_PTR(m_action);\n"
"\n"
"       m_action->setToolTip(i18n(\"Draw a star\"));\n"
"       m_action->setExclusiveGroup(\"tools\");\n"
"       m_ownAction = true;\n"
"   }\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:742
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>starCoordinates()</methodname> method contains some funky "
"math &mdash; but is not too interesting for the discussion of how to create "
"a tool plugins."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:748
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KisPoint KisToolStar::starCoordinates(int N, double mx, double my, double x, "
"double y)\n"
"{\n"
"   double R=0, r=0;\n"
"   qint32 n=0;\n"
"   double angle;\n"
"\n"
"   vKisPoint starCoordinatesArray(2*N);\n"
"\n"
"   // the radius of the outer edges\n"
"   R=sqrt((x-mx)*(x-mx)+(y-my)*(y-my));\n"
"\n"
"   // the radius of the inner edges\n"
"   r=R*m_innerOuterRatio/100.0;\n"
"\n"
"   // the angle\n"
"   angle=-atan2((x-mx),(y-my));\n"
"\n"
"   //set outer edges\n"
"   for(n=0;n&lt;N;n++){\n"
"       starCoordinatesArray[2*n] = KisPoint(mx+R*cos(n * 2.0 * M_PI / N + "
"angle),my+R*sin(n *2.0 * M_PI / N+angle));\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   //set inner edges\n"
"   for(n=0;n&lt;N;n++){\n"
"       starCoordinatesArray[2*n+1] = KisPoint(mx+r*cos((n + 0.5) * 2.0 * "
"M_PI / N + angle),my+r*sin((n +0.5) * 2.0 * M_PI / N + angle));\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   return starCoordinatesArray;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:750
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>createOptionWidget()</methodname> method is called to create "
"the option widget that &krita; will show in the tab. Since there is a tool "
"per input device per view, the state of a tool can be kept in the tool. This "
"method is only called once: the option widget is stored and retrieved the "
"next time the tool is activated."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:758
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"QWidget* KisToolStar::createOptionWidget(QWidget* parent)\n"
"{\n"
"   QWidget *widget = KisToolShape::createOptionWidget(parent);\n"
"\n"
"   m_optWidget = new WdgToolStar(widget);\n"
"   Q_CHECK_PTR(m_optWidget);\n"
"\n"
"   m_optWidget->ratioSpinBox->setValue(m_innerOuterRatio);\n"
"\n"
"   QGridLayout *optionLayout = new QGridLayout(widget, 1, 1);\n"
"   super::addOptionWidgetLayout(optionLayout);\n"
"\n"
"   optionLayout->addWidget(m_optWidget, 0, 0);\n"
"\n"
"   return widget;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:761
#, no-c-format
msgid "Tool Conclusions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:763
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Tools are relatively simple plugins to create. You need to combine the "
"<classname>KisTool</classname> and <classname>KisCanvasObserver</classname> "
"interfaces in order to effectively create a tool."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:774
#, no-c-format
msgid "Paint operations"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:776
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"PaintOps are one of the more innovative types of plugins in Krita (together "
"with pluggable colorspaces). A paint operation defines how tools change the "
"pixels they touch. Airbrush, aliased pencil or antialiased pixel brush: "
"these are all paint operations. But you could &mdash; with a lot of work "
"&mdash; create a paintop that reads Corel Painter XML brush definitions and "
"uses those to determine how painting is done."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:783
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Paint operations are instantiated when a paint tool receives a "
"<literal>mouseDown</literal> event and are deleted when the mouseUp event is "
"received by a paint tool. In between, the paintop can keep track of previous "
"positions and other data, such as pressure levels if the user uses a tablet."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:788
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The basic operation of a paint operation is to change pixels at the cursor "
"position of a paint tool. That can be done only once, or the paint op can "
"demand to be run at regular intervals, using a timer. The first would be "
"useful for a pencil-type paint op, the second, of course, for an airbrush-"
"type paintop."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:794
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Paintops can have a small configuration widget which is placed in a toolbar. "
"Thus, paintop configuration widgets need to have a horizontal layout of "
"widgets that are not higher than a toolbar button. Otherwise, &krita; will "
"look very funny."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:799
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let’s look at a simple paintop plugin, one that shows a little bit of "
"programmatic intelligence. First, in the header file, there’s a factory "
"defined. This factory creates a paintop when the active tool needs one:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:805
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"public:\n"
"   KisSmearyOpFactory() {}\n"
"   virtual ~KisSmearyOpFactory() {}\n"
"\n"
"   virtual KisPaintOp * createOp(const KisPaintOpSettings *settings, "
"KisPainter * painter);\n"
"   virtual KisID id() { return KisID(\"paintSmeary\", i18n(\"Smeary Brush"
"\")); }\n"
"   virtual bool userVisible(KisColorSpace * ) { return false; }\n"
"   virtual QString pixmap() { return \"\"; }\n"
"\n"
"};"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:807
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The factory also contains the <classname>KisID</classname> with the public "
"and private name for the paintop &mdash; make sure your paintop’s private "
"name does not clash with another paintop! &mdash; and may optionally return "
"a pixmap. &krita; can then show the pixmap together with the name for visual "
"identification of your paintop. For instance, a painter’s knife paintop "
"would have the image of such an implement."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:814
#, no-c-format
msgid "The implementation of a paintop is very straightforward:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:818
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KisSmearyOp::KisSmearyOp(KisPainter * painter)\n"
"   : KisPaintOp(painter)\n"
"{\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"KisSmearyOp::~KisSmearyOp()\n"
"{\n"
"}\n"
"void KisSmearyOp::paintAt(const KisPoint &amp;pos, const "
"KisPaintInformation&amp; info)\n"
"{"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:820
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <methodname>paintAt()</methodname> method really is where it’s at, with "
"paintops. This method receives two parameters: the current position (which "
"is in floats, not in whole pixels) and a <classname>KisPaintInformation</"
"classname> object. which contains the pressure, x and y tilt, and movement "
"vector, and may in the future be extended with other information."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:829
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if (!m_painter->device()) return;\n"
"\n"
"   KisBrush *brush = m_painter->brush();"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:831
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A <classname>KisBrush</classname> is the representation of a Gimp brush "
"file: that is a mask, either a single mask or a series of masks. Actually, "
"we don’t use the brush here, except to determine the <quote>hotspot</quote> "
"under the cursor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:838
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Q_ASSERT(brush);\n"
"\n"
"   if (!brush) return;\n"
"\n"
"   if (! brush->canPaintFor(info) )\n"
"       return;\n"
"\n"
"   KisPaintDeviceSP device = m_painter->device();\n"
"   KisColorSpace * colorSpace = device->colorSpace();\n"
"   KisColor kc = m_painter->paintColor();\n"
"   kc.convertTo(colorSpace);\n"
"\n"
"   KisPoint hotSpot = brush->hotSpot(info);\n"
"   KisPoint pt = pos - hotSpot;\n"
"\n"
"   // Split the coordinates into integer plus fractional parts. The integer\n"
"   // is where the dab will be positioned and the fractional part "
"determines\n"
"   // the sub-pixel positioning.\n"
"   qint32 x, y;\n"
"   double xFraction, yFraction;\n"
"\n"
"   splitCoordinate(pt.x(), &amp;x, &amp;xFraction);\n"
"   splitCoordinate(pt.y(), &amp;y, &amp;yFraction);\n"
"\n"
"   KisPaintDeviceSP dab = new KisPaintDevice(colorSpace, \"smeary dab\");\n"
"   Q_CHECK_PTR(dab);"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:840
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"We don’t change the pixels of a paint device directly: instead we create a "
"small paint device, a dab, and composite that onto the current paint device."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:845
#, no-c-format
msgid "m_painter->setPressure(info.pressure);"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:847
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As the comments say, the next bit code does some programmatic work to create "
"the actual dab. In this case, we draw a number of lines. When I am done with "
"this paintop, the length, position and thickness of the lines will be "
"dependent on pressure and paint load, and we’ll have create a stiff, smeary "
"oilpaint brush. But I haven’t had time to finish this yet."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:855
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"// Compute the position of the tufts. The tufts are arranged in a line\n"
"   // perpendicular to the motion of the brush, i.e, the straight line "
"between\n"
"   // the current position and the previous position.\n"
"   // The tufts are spread out through the pressure\n"
"\n"
"   KisPoint previousPoint = info.movement.toKisPoint();\n"
"   KisVector2D brushVector(-previousPoint.y(), previousPoint.x());\n"
"   KisVector2D currentPointVector = KisVector2D(pos);\n"
"   brushVector.normalize();\n"
"\n"
"   KisVector2D vl, vr;\n"
"\n"
"   for (int i = 0; i &lt; (NUMBER_OF_TUFTS / 2); ++i) {\n"
"       // Compute the positions on the new vector.\n"
"       vl = currentPointVector + i * brushVector;\n"
"       KisPoint pl = vl.toKisPoint();\n"
"       dab->setPixel(pl.roundX(), pl.roundY(), kc);\n"
"\n"
"       vr = currentPointVector - i * brushVector;\n"
"       KisPoint pr = vr.toKisPoint();\n"
"       dab->setPixel(pr.roundX(), pr.roundY(), kc);\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   vr = vr - vl;\n"
"   vr.normalize();"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:857
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Finally we blt the dab onto the original paint device and tell the painter "
"that we’ve dirtied a small rectangle of the paint device."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:862
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"if (m_source->hasSelection()) {\n"
"       m_painter->bltSelection(x - 32, y - 32, m_painter->compositeOp(), dab."
"data(),\n"
"                               m_source->selection(), m_painter->opacity(), "
"x - 32, y -32, 64, 64);\n"
"   }\n"
"   else {\n"
"       m_painter->bitBlt(x - 32, y - 32, m_painter->compositeOp(), dab.data"
"(), m_painter->opacity(), x - 32, y -32, 64, 64);\n"
"   }\n"
"\n"
"   m_painter->addDirtyRect(QRect(x -32, y -32, 64, 64));\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"KisPaintOp * KisSmearyOpFactory::createOp(const KisPaintOpSettings */"
"*settings*/, KisPainter * painter)\n"
"{\n"
"   KisPaintOp * op = new KisSmearyOp(painter);\n"
"   Q_CHECK_PTR(op);\n"
"   return op;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:864
#, no-c-format
msgid "That’s all: paintops are easy and fun!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:871
#, no-c-format
msgid "View plugins"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:873
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"View plugins are the weirdest of the bunch: a view plugin is an ordinary "
"KPart that can provide a bit of user interface and some functionality. For "
"instance, the histogram tab is a view plugin, as is the rotate dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:884
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&krita; works with the ordinary &koffice; file filter architecture. There is "
"a tutorial, a bit old, but still useful, at: <ulink url=\"http://koffice.org/"
"developer/filters/oldfaq.php\"></ulink>. It is probably best to cooperate "
"with the &krita; team when developing file filters and do the development in "
"the &koffice; filter tree. Note that you can test your filters without "
"running &krita; using the <command>koconverter</command> utility."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:890
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Filters have two sides: importing and exporting. These are usually two "
"different plugins that may share some code."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:893
#, no-c-format
msgid "The important <filename>Makefile.am</filename> entries are:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:897
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"service_DATA = krita_XXX_import.desktop krita_XXX_export.desktop\n"
"servicedir = $(kde_servicesdir)\n"
"kdelnk_DATA = krita_XXX.desktop\n"
"kdelnkdir = $(kde_appsdir)/Office\n"
"libkritaXXXimport_la_SOURCES = XXXimport.cpp\n"
"libkritaXXXexport_la_SOURCES = XXXexport.cpp\n"
"METASOURCES = AUTO"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:899
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Whether you are building an import filter or an export filter, your work "
"always boils down to implementing the following function:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:904
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"virtual KoFilter::ConversionStatus convert(const QCString&amp; from, const "
"QCString&amp; to);"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:906
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"It is the settings in the <literal role=\"extension\">.desktop</literal> "
"files that determine which way a filter converts:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:909
#, no-c-format
msgid "Import:"
msgstr "Importar :"

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:913
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"X-KDE-Export=application/x-krita\n"
"X-KDE-Import=image/x-xcf-gimp\n"
"X-KDE-Weight=1\n"
"X-KDE-Library=libkritaXXXimport\n"
"ServiceTypes=KOfficeFilter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:915
#, no-c-format
msgid "Export:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:919
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"X-KDE-Export=image/x-xcf-gimp\n"
"X-KDE-Import=application/x-krita\n"
"ServiceTypes=KOfficeFilter\n"
"Type=Service\n"
"X-KDE-Weight=1\n"
"X-KDE-Library=libkritaXXXexport"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:921
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"And yes, the mimetype chosen for the example is a hint. Please, pretty "
"please, implement an xcf filter?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: developers-plugins.docbook:927
#, no-c-format
msgid "Import"
msgstr "Importar"

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:929
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The big problem with import filters is of course your code to read the data "
"on disk. The boilerplate for calling that code is fairly simple:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:934
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note: we really, really should find a way to enable &krita; to keep a file "
"open and only read data on a as-needed basis, instead of copying the entire "
"contents to the internal paint device representation. But that would mean "
"datamanager backends that know about tiff files and so on, and is not "
"currently implemented. It would be ideal if some file filters could "
"implement a class provisionally named <classname>KisFileDataManager</"
"classname>, create an object of that instance with the current file and pass "
"that to KisDoc. But &krita; handles storage per layer, not per document, so "
"this would be a hard refactor to do."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: developers-plugins.docbook:944
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"KoFilter::ConversionStatus XXXImport::convert(const QCString&amp;, const "
"QCString&amp; to)\n"
"{\n"
"   if (to != \"application/x-krita\") <co id=\"import1\" />\n"
"       return KoFilter::BadMimeType;\n"
"\n"
"   KisDoc * doc = dynamic_cast&lt;KisDoc*&gt;(m_chain -> outputDocument()); "
"<co id=\"import2\" />\n"
"   KisView * view = static_cast&lt;KisView*&gt;(doc -> views().getFirst()); "
"<co id=\"import3\" />\n"
"   \n"
"   QString filename = m_chain -> inputFile(); <co id=\"import4\" />\n"
"   \n"
"   if (!doc)\n"
"       return KoFilter::CreationError;\n"
"\n"
"   doc -> prepareForImport(); <co id=\"import5\" />\n"
"\n"
"   if (!filename.isEmpty()) {\n"
"   \n"
"       KURL url(filename);\n"
"\n"
"       if (url.isEmpty())\n"
"           return KoFilter::FileNotFound;\n"
"           \n"
"       KisImageXXXConverter ib(doc, doc -> undoAdapter()); <co id=\"import6"
"\" />\n"
"\n"
"       if (view != 0)\n"
"           view -> canvasSubject() ->  progressDisplay() -> setSubject(&amp;"
"ib, false, true);\n"
"\n"
"       switch (ib.buildImage(url)) <co id=\"import7\" /> {\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_UNSUPPORTED:\n"
"               return KoFilter::NotImplemented;\n"
"               break;\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_INVALID_ARG:\n"
"               return KoFilter::BadMimeType;\n"
"               break;\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_NO_URI:\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_NOT_LOCAL:\n"
"               return KoFilter::FileNotFound;\n"
"               break;\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_BAD_FETCH:\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_EMPTY:\n"
"               return KoFilter::ParsingError;                \n"
"               break;\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_FAILURE:\n"
"               return KoFilter::InternalError;\n"
"               break;\n"
"           case KisImageBuilder_RESULT_OK:\n"
"               doc -> setCurrentImage( ib.image()); <co id=\"import8\" />\n"
"               return KoFilter::OK;\n"
"           default:\n"
"               break;\n"
"       }\n"
"\n"
"   }\n"
"   return KoFilter::StorageCreationError;\n"
"}"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:947
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is supposed to be an importfilter, so if it is not called to convert to "
"a &krita; image, then something is wrong."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:950
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The filter chain already has created an output document for us. We need to "
"cast it to <classname>KisDocM</classname>, because &krita; documents need "
"special treatment. It would not, actually, be all that bad an idea to check "
"whether the result of the cast is not 0, because if it is, importing will "
"fail."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:955
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If we call this filter from the GUI, we try to get the view. If there is a "
"view, the conversion code can try to update the progressbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:958
#, no-c-format
msgid "The filter has the filename for our input file for us."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:960
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<classname>KisDoc</classname> needs to be prepared for import. Certain "
"settings are initialized and undo is disabled. Otherwise you could undo the "
"adding of layers performed by the import filter and that is weird behaviour."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:964
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"I have chosen to implement the actual importing code in a separate class "
"that I instantiate here. You can also put all your code right in this "
"method, but that would be a bit messy."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:968
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"My importer returns a statuscode that I can then use to set the status of "
"the import filter. &koffice; takes care of showing error messages."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: developers-plugins.docbook:971
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If creating the <classname>KisImage</classname> has succeeded we set the "
"document's current image to our newly created image. Then we are done: "
"<literal>return KoFilter::OK;</literal>."
msgstr ""
